1
00:00:00,150 --> 00:00:02,869
Everything in this
painting really happened.

2
00:00:03,869 --> 00:00:07,849
Men on a raft, waving at
a ship you can barely see.

3
00:00:07,869 --> 00:00:09,994
Around them, the dead.

4
00:00:10,864 --> 00:00:13,004
Thirteen days before this moment,

5
00:00:13,024 --> 00:00:16,284
nearly a hundred and fifty
people were on this raft.

6
00:00:16,304 --> 00:00:19,451
When rescue came, fifteen were alive.

7
00:00:20,321 --> 00:00:25,181
To paint it, a young man shaved his
head, shut himself in his studio,

8
00:00:25,201 --> 00:00:27,470
and filled it with pieces of the dead.

9
00:00:28,340 --> 00:00:31,280
This is the story of
the Raft of the Medusa.

10
00:00:31,300 --> 00:00:35,433
The shipwreck France wanted to forget,
and the painting that wouldn't let it.

11
00:00:37,693 --> 00:00:39,403
June, 1816.

12
00:00:39,693 --> 00:00:41,353
Napoleon is gone.

13
00:00:41,373 --> 00:00:43,833
A king sits on the French throne again.

14
00:00:43,853 --> 00:00:45,379
Louis the Eighteenth.

15
00:00:45,989 --> 00:00:51,649
Under the peace treaties, France gets back
its colony in Senegal, in West Africa.

16
00:00:51,669 --> 00:00:55,015
A small convoy sails from
Rochefort to take it back.

17
00:00:55,625 --> 00:00:59,045
Leading it is a navy frigate, the Méduse.

18
00:00:59,065 --> 00:01:00,375
The Medusa.

19
00:01:00,665 --> 00:01:04,885
On board: the new governor,
officials, soldiers, settlers.

20
00:01:04,905 --> 00:01:06,689
Around four hundred people.

21
00:01:07,429 --> 00:01:10,129
The captain is Hugues Duroy de Chaumareys.

22
00:01:10,149 --> 00:01:14,209
A nobleman who fled the Revolution
and fought for the crown.

23
00:01:14,229 --> 00:01:16,849
He has hardly sailed in twenty years.

24
00:01:16,869 --> 00:01:21,384
He gets the command anyway, reportedly
for his loyalty to the king.

25
00:01:22,124 --> 00:01:24,264
He races ahead of the other ships.

26
00:01:24,284 --> 00:01:28,904
He lets a passenger with no naval
training help guide the ship.

27
00:01:28,924 --> 00:01:33,179
The Medusa ends up about a hundred
and sixty kilometres off course.

28
00:01:33,919 --> 00:01:38,059
On the second of July, off the
coast of what is now Mauritania,

29
00:01:38,079 --> 00:01:40,779
the Medusa runs onto a sandbank.

30
00:01:40,799 --> 00:01:42,699
The Arguin Bank.

31
00:01:42,719 --> 00:01:45,084
And there, she sticks fast.

32
00:01:46,984 --> 00:01:48,644
There are six boats.

33
00:01:48,664 --> 00:01:51,208
Not nearly enough for four hundred people.

34
00:01:51,818 --> 00:01:55,878
So the crew builds a raft out
of the ship's masts and spars.

35
00:01:55,898 --> 00:01:57,679
About twenty metres long.

36
00:01:58,289 --> 00:02:03,851
On the fifth of July, a hundred and
forty-six men and one woman climb onto it.

37
00:02:04,331 --> 00:02:09,271
Their supplies: one bag of ship's
biscuit, gone on the first day.

38
00:02:09,291 --> 00:02:10,951
Two casks of water.

39
00:02:10,971 --> 00:02:12,598
Six casks of wine.

40
00:02:13,258 --> 00:02:16,628
Seventeen men refuse to
leave the ship at all.

41
00:02:16,648 --> 00:02:18,868
The captain does not stay with them.

42
00:02:18,888 --> 00:02:21,262
He takes his place in one of the boats.

43
00:02:21,872 --> 00:02:25,292
The plan is that the boats
will tow the raft to the coast.

44
00:02:25,312 --> 00:02:29,004
After only a few miles,
the raft is cut loose.

45
00:02:29,854 --> 00:02:30,874
No rudder.

46
00:02:30,894 --> 00:02:32,314
No way to steer.

47
00:02:32,334 --> 00:02:33,943
The boats row away.

48
00:02:35,843 --> 00:02:38,063
The first night, the sea gets up.

49
00:02:38,083 --> 00:02:40,383
Men tie themselves to the timbers.

50
00:02:40,403 --> 00:02:45,583
One of them wrote later: This whole
night we contended against death.

51
00:02:45,603 --> 00:02:48,304
By morning, twenty men are gone.

52
00:02:49,044 --> 00:02:52,304
The next night, soldiers
break into the wine.

53
00:02:52,324 --> 00:02:55,874
Drunk and desperate, they
turn on the officers.

54
00:02:55,937 --> 00:02:57,024
Sabres.

55
00:02:57,044 --> 00:02:58,147
Knives.

56
00:02:58,167 --> 00:02:59,584
An axe.

57
00:02:59,604 --> 00:03:01,775
By morning, dozens are dead.

58
00:03:02,515 --> 00:03:05,775
By the fourth day, sixty-seven are left.

59
00:03:05,795 --> 00:03:07,855
They catch a few flying fish.

60
00:03:07,875 --> 00:03:09,775
It isn't nearly enough.

61
00:03:09,795 --> 00:03:13,160
And the survivors do what
they swore they never would.

62
00:03:13,770 --> 00:03:18,390
The ship's surgeon, Henri
Savigny, wrote it down himself.

63
00:03:18,410 --> 00:03:22,091
Those whom death had spared
fell upon the dead bodies.

64
00:03:22,961 --> 00:03:26,541
On the eighth day, the
strongest make a decision.

65
00:03:26,561 --> 00:03:29,661
The sick and the wounded will not survive.

66
00:03:29,681 --> 00:03:32,061
So they are thrown into the sea.

67
00:03:32,081 --> 00:03:34,011
Fifteen men are left.

68
00:03:35,041 --> 00:03:36,340
Five more days.

69
00:03:36,950 --> 00:03:41,330
On the seventeenth of July,
someone sees a sail on the horizon.

70
00:03:41,350 --> 00:03:42,370
A ship.

71
00:03:42,390 --> 00:03:43,780
The Argus.

72
00:03:43,990 --> 00:03:45,170
They wave.

73
00:03:45,190 --> 00:03:46,370
They shout.

74
00:03:46,390 --> 00:03:48,260
And the ship disappears.

75
00:03:49,149 --> 00:03:52,169
For two hours, they think it's over.

76
00:03:52,189 --> 00:03:54,090
Then the Argus comes back.

77
00:03:54,990 --> 00:03:56,810
Fifteen men are alive.

78
00:03:56,830 --> 00:03:58,970
Five of them die soon after.

79
00:03:58,990 --> 00:04:03,309
Of the seventeen who stayed on the
wreck, only three are found alive.

80
00:04:05,209 --> 00:04:08,238
News of the wreck reaches
Paris in September.

81
00:04:08,258 --> 00:04:11,029
Savigny has written a report for the navy.

82
00:04:11,049 --> 00:04:12,932
It doesn't stay with the navy.

83
00:04:13,412 --> 00:04:17,065
It leaks to a Paris newspaper
hostile to the government.

84
00:04:17,545 --> 00:04:20,645
Paris reads about the captain
who left his ship early,

85
00:04:20,665 --> 00:04:25,224
the boats that cut the raft loose, and
the men who ate the dead to stay alive.

86
00:04:25,964 --> 00:04:29,274
For the new king's government,
it's a humiliation.

87
00:04:29,856 --> 00:04:32,266
Chaumareys faces a court-martial.

88
00:04:32,286 --> 00:04:37,146
He's found guilty of incompetent
navigation, and of abandoning his ship

89
00:04:37,166 --> 00:04:39,546
before all its passengers were off.

90
00:04:39,566 --> 00:04:41,706
He could have been sentenced to death.

91
00:04:41,726 --> 00:04:43,828
He gets three years in prison.

92
00:04:44,568 --> 00:04:50,650
In 1817, Savigny and another survivor,
the engineer Alexandre Corréard,

93
00:04:50,670 --> 00:04:52,788
publish their story as a book.

94
00:04:52,808 --> 00:04:56,313
It becomes a bestseller,
translated across Europe.

95
00:04:56,793 --> 00:04:59,904
And in Paris, a young painter reads it.

96
00:05:01,853 --> 00:05:04,423
Théodore Géricault is twenty-six.

97
00:05:04,443 --> 00:05:07,783
Rich, gifted, and in trouble.

98
00:05:07,803 --> 00:05:11,443
He has just broken off a love
affair with his own aunt.

99
00:05:12,053 --> 00:05:14,353
He wants to paint something enormous.

100
00:05:14,373 --> 00:05:16,152
He chooses the Medusa.

101
00:05:16,762 --> 00:05:18,740
He tracks down the survivors.

102
00:05:18,760 --> 00:05:20,693
Savigny and Corréard.

103
00:05:20,762 --> 00:05:25,273
Even the ship's carpenter, who helps
him build a scale model of the raft.

104
00:05:25,883 --> 00:05:29,463
Then he shaves his head, and stays inside.

105
00:05:29,483 --> 00:05:34,112
For the next eight months, he lives
in his studio almost like a monk.

106
00:05:34,722 --> 00:05:38,782
The studio is close to a
hospital, the Hôpital Beaujon.

107
00:05:38,802 --> 00:05:42,142
He goes to its morgue, and draws the dead.

108
00:05:42,162 --> 00:05:48,468
He brings arms and legs back to his studio,
to watch how skin changes as it decays.

109
00:05:49,078 --> 00:05:52,978
One account says he borrowed
a severed head from an asylum,

110
00:05:52,998 --> 00:05:55,675
and kept it on his studio
roof for two weeks.

111
00:05:56,416 --> 00:05:58,316
These are some of his studies.

112
00:05:58,336 --> 00:06:02,686
Today they hang in museums, in
Stockholm, and in Montpellier.

113
00:06:03,394 --> 00:06:05,134
Friends come to pose.

114
00:06:05,154 --> 00:06:09,214
The young Eugène Delacroix lies
face down in the foreground.

115
00:06:09,234 --> 00:06:12,334
Savigny and Corréard pose as themselves.

116
00:06:12,354 --> 00:06:16,127
And one of his assistants
poses as the dead young man.

117
00:06:16,737 --> 00:06:19,117
He makes more than a hundred studies.

118
00:06:19,137 --> 00:06:20,877
He tries the mutiny.

119
00:06:20,897 --> 00:06:22,767
He tries the cannibalism.

120
00:06:22,897 --> 00:06:24,717
He tries the rescue.

121
00:06:24,737 --> 00:06:27,528
And in the end, he paints none of them.

122
00:06:29,428 --> 00:06:31,488
He paints the moment of hope.

123
00:06:31,508 --> 00:06:35,165
The first sight of the Argus,
a speck on the horizon.

124
00:06:35,905 --> 00:06:41,135
At the top, a Black man stands on an
empty barrel, waving a handkerchief.

125
00:06:41,345 --> 00:06:46,445
On the real raft, one of the fifteen
was a soldier named Jean Charles.

126
00:06:46,465 --> 00:06:49,215
He died within days of the rescue.

127
00:06:49,438 --> 00:06:54,365
Géricault painted him from a model named
Joseph, a well-known model in Paris,

128
00:06:54,385 --> 00:06:55,964
of Haitian descent.

129
00:06:56,864 --> 00:07:02,481
Below him, the bodies rise like a
pyramid, from the dead, up to the living.

130
00:07:03,091 --> 00:07:08,191
In the corner, an old man holds a
young man's body across his knees.

131
00:07:08,211 --> 00:07:09,711
He isn't waving.

132
00:07:09,731 --> 00:07:12,111
He isn't even looking at the ship.

133
00:07:12,131 --> 00:07:14,191
The pose comes from Dante.

134
00:07:14,211 --> 00:07:19,033
Count Ugolino, locked in a tower
with his sons while they starved.

135
00:07:19,903 --> 00:07:24,522
The canvas is almost five metres
high, and more than seven metres wide.

136
00:07:25,262 --> 00:07:29,162
In 1819, it goes on
show at the Paris Salon.

137
00:07:29,182 --> 00:07:31,404
Its title gives nothing away.

138
00:07:31,424 --> 00:07:33,082
Scène de naufrage.

139
00:07:33,102 --> 00:07:34,602
Shipwreck Scene.

140
00:07:34,622 --> 00:07:36,635
Everyone knows which shipwreck.

141
00:07:37,375 --> 00:07:39,195
Some critics are shaken.

142
00:07:39,215 --> 00:07:41,645
Others call it a pile of corpses.

143
00:07:41,775 --> 00:07:43,675
The king, it's said,

144
00:07:43,695 --> 00:07:47,934
told the painter that his shipwreck
was certainly no disaster for him.

145
00:07:48,674 --> 00:07:50,334
It wins a gold medal.

146
00:07:50,354 --> 00:07:52,654
But the state doesn't buy it.

147
00:07:52,674 --> 00:07:55,454
One young painter is
struck hardest of all.

148
00:07:55,474 --> 00:07:59,019
Delacroix, the man lying
face down in the corner.

149
00:07:59,759 --> 00:08:01,979
So Géricault takes it to London.

150
00:08:01,999 --> 00:08:08,189
In 1820, about forty thousand people
pay to see it in a hall on Piccadilly.

151
00:08:08,239 --> 00:08:10,930
It earns him nearly
twenty thousand francs.

152
00:08:12,880 --> 00:08:16,170
Horse-riding accidents
and illness wear him down.

153
00:08:16,190 --> 00:08:20,970
In January 1824, Théodore
Géricault dies in Paris.

154
00:08:20,990 --> 00:08:22,540
He is thirty-two.

155
00:08:23,454 --> 00:08:27,218
That same year, the Louvre
buys the Raft from his family.

156
00:08:27,828 --> 00:08:29,968
But the painting has a problem.

157
00:08:29,988 --> 00:08:35,088
For its deep shadows, Géricault
used bitumen, a kind of tar.

158
00:08:35,108 --> 00:08:38,768
Over the years it turned
black, and wrinkled the paint.

159
00:08:38,788 --> 00:08:40,454
It can't be reversed.

160
00:08:41,224 --> 00:08:44,404
Whole areas of the picture
are now hard to see.

161
00:08:44,424 --> 00:08:47,547
The raft is sinking, one more time,

162
00:08:48,807 --> 00:08:51,027
Look at the horizon again.

163
00:08:51,047 --> 00:08:53,138
That tiny ship is still there.

164
00:08:53,538 --> 00:08:57,043
Two hundred years later,
they're still waving at it.

165
00:08:58,043 --> 00:09:00,664
It hangs in the Louvre, in Paris.
